@node what "What it is"
@toc main
@next need
@{b}@{u}What it is@{uu}@{ub}

A dock along one edge of your Workbench screen.

Click an icon to start its program; drag icons on from Workbench to
add them. The dock shows which programs are running, brings their
windows to the front, and can hide itself until you need it.

@{i}Next: @{"What you need" link need}@{ui}
@endnode

@node need "What you need"
@toc main
@prev what
@next install
@{b}@{u}What you need@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}Minimum@{ub}
  AmigaOS 3.2, or 3.1.4, 3.5, 3.9, or a 3.1-based system that has
  workbench.library and icon.library 44 or newer.
  A 68030 or better, with Fast RAM. A stock 68020 A1200 works, slowly.

@{b}Best@{ub}
  A 68040 or 68060, or a PiStorm, and a graphics card with a 16- or
  24-bit screen (Picasso96). AGA screens work, more slowly.

@{i}Without ReAction's chooser.gadget (before 3.2) the lists in the@{ui}
@{i}settings window are cycle gadgets: a click steps to the next choice.@{ui}
@endnode

@node install "Installing"
@toc main
@prev need
@next five
@{b}@{u}Installing@{uu}@{ub}

Put the SimpleDock drawer anywhere and double-click @{b}SimpleDock@{ub}. There
is no installer.

To start it with Workbench, put a copy of its icon in @{b}SYS:WBStartup@{ub}.

The first start creates @{b}ENVARC:SimpleDock.config@{ub} and fills the dock
with the standard icons it finds - the Workbench disk, Shell, MultiView,
Clock, the Prefs, Tools and Utilities drawers, Ram Disk - as many as fit.

@{b}To remove it@{ub}, delete the drawer, ENVARC:SimpleDock.config, and the
copy in WBStartup if you made one.
@endnode

@node five "The five things"
@toc main
@prev install
@next running
@{b}@{u}The five things@{uu}@{ub}

  @{b}Click an icon@{ub}        starts the program (or opens the drawer, disk
                         or document). Already running: its windows
                         come to the front instead.
  @{b}Drag an icon on@{ub}      from Workbench, between two dock icons: it is
                         added there.
  @{b}Drag an icon off@{ub}     the dock, anywhere: it is removed from the
                         dock (not from the disk).
  @{b}Drag an icon along@{ub}   the dock: it moves; the others make way.
  @{b}Ctrl+Alt+Shift@{ub}       pressed together and let go: shows the dock
                         when it is hidden, or brings it to the front.

Everything you do with the mouse is saved as you do it.

The settings window is in the @{b}Dock@{ub} menu of the Workbench menu bar,
with About and Quit: see @{"The simple side" link simple}.

@{i}That is all you need. The rest of this guide is for when you want more.@{ui}
@endnode

@node running "Running programs"
@toc main
@prev five
@next longpress
@{b}@{u}Running programs@{uu}@{ub}

A @{b}dot@{ub} under an icon means its program is running; two copies or more
show their number on the icon.

A program that is @{b}not on the dock@{ub} shows up too, at the end behind a
thin line, with its own icon, for as long as it runs. Open Shells are
one Shell icon.

  @{b}Click@{ub}            all the program's windows to the front; click
                     again to go through them one at a time
  @{b}Shift+click@{ub}      another copy of the program
  @{b}Del@{ub}              with the pointer over the icon: closes the program
                     (its windows are asked to close, as their close
                     gadget would; a program may ask you first)

To @{b}keep@{ub} a running program on the dock, drag its icon in front of the
line. To @{b}never see@{ub} one there, drag it off: the dock asks, then leaves
it out. Hidden programs in the settings window (@{"Programs" link programs})
lists them and brings one back.

While Workbench has drawer windows open, a @{b}Workbench icon@{ub} sits right
after the line and counts them; a click brings them all to the front.
A docked drawer that is open gets a dot of its own.

@{i}See also: @{"Long press" link longpress}, @{"Force Quit" link forcequit}@{ui}
@endnode

@node longpress "Long press"
@toc main
@prev running
@next autohide
@{b}@{u}Long press@{uu}@{ub}

Hold the button on a running program's icon for a moment and a list of
its windows opens. Let go over a window to bring it to the front. Let
go anywhere else and the list stays open:

  @{b}click a window@{ub}       brings it to the front
  @{b}point, press Del@{ub}     closes that window
  @{b}below the line@{ub}       what can be done with the program:

    @{b}Keep in Dock@{ub} / @{b}Remove from Dock@{ub}
    @{b}Show in Workbench@{ub}    opens its drawer
    @{b}Minimise@{ub}             hides its windows; the dot pulses.
                           @{b}Show (n)@{ub} brings them back - so does a
                           click on the icon.
    @{b}Close@{ub}                closes it
    @{b}Force Quit...@{ub}        appears once Close did not work:
                           see @{"Force Quit" link forcequit}

  @{b}Esc@{ub}, or a click elsewhere, closes the list.

With several windows, Minimise, Show and Close end in "..." and the
list asks which one, or All.

@{i}To drag the icon instead of opening the list, keep the button down@{ui}
@{i}and slide along the dock.@{ui}
@endnode

@node autohide "Auto-hide"
@toc main
@prev longpress
@next drops
@{b}@{u}Auto-hide@{uu}@{ub}

With auto-hide on (settings: Auto-hide), the dock hides itself and
shows again when the pointer touches that edge of the screen.

The @{b}hotkey@{ub} (Ctrl+Alt+Shift) shows it and keeps it shown until the
pointer has visited it and left; pressed while the dock is shown, it
hides it. @{b}Ctrl+Alt+H@{ub} switches auto-hide on or off (@{"Keys" link keys}).

@{i}With auto-hide, icons cannot be dropped onto the hidden dock, because@{ui}
@{i}Workbench locks the screen during a drag. Press the hotkey first.@{ui}
@endnode

@node drops "Dropping files"
@toc main
@prev autohide
@next missing
@{b}@{u}Dropping files@{uu}@{ub}

Drag icons from Workbench:

  @{b}onto a program icon@{ub}     they are opened with it - a text file onto
                            your editor
  @{b}onto a drawer or disk@{ub}   they are copied into it, with their icons,
                            dates and comments. A file already there:
                            Replace or Skip. Drawers are never merged.
  @{b}between two icons@{ub}       they join the dock
  @{b}into a Shell window@{ub}     their paths are typed: see @{"Shell drops" link shelldrops}

A @{b}beep@{ub} means a drop was refused: a drawer into itself, a whole disk,
the Trash, or a full dock.

@{i}"Drop on a drawer" in the settings (@{"Programs" link programs}) can make@{ui}
@{i}drops move the files instead, when they are on the same disk.@{ui}
@endnode

@node missing "Missing icons"
@toc main
@prev drops
@next keys
@{b}@{u}Missing icons@{uu}@{ub}

An icon is dimmed and marked @{b}?@{ub} when its file or disk is not there.
Click it to be asked whether to remove it.

The dock never removes it by itself: a disk may just not be inserted.
@endnode

@node keys "Keys"
@toc main
@prev missing
@next shelldrops
@{b}@{u}Keys@{uu}@{ub}

  @{b}Ctrl+Alt+Shift@{ub}      shows the dock (@{"The five things" link five})
  @{b}Ctrl+Alt+M@{ub}          minimises the active window, like Minimise in
                        the long-press list
  @{b}Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M@{ub}    minimises all, by what is active:
                          a drawer window      all drawer windows
                          a program's window   all its windows (every
                                               copy; a Shell: all Shells)
                          nothing, or the      every window
                          Workbench backdrop
                        With nothing left showing, the same key brings
                        them all back.
  @{b}Ctrl+Alt+H@{ub}          auto-hide on or off (kept in the settings)

Change any of them under Keys (@{"Shell drops and Keys" link advshell}); NONE
switches one off.

@{i}A program that used one of these combinations no longer gets it@{ui}
@{i}while the dock runs.@{ui}
@endnode

@node shelldrops "Shell drops"
@toc main
@prev keys
@next forcequit
@{b}@{u}Shell drops@{uu}@{ub}

Drag icons from Workbench into a Shell window and their *paths are
typed* at the cursor, in quotes where a name has a space:

    "Ram Disk:" Work:Music

Nothing is run; add to the line or press Return yourself.

Dropped @{b}onto the dock's Shell icon@{ub}, they go into the Shell window in
front, which is brought to the front; with no Shell open, a new one
starts and gets them.

A single @{b}archive@{ub} gets the command that unpacks it, if its tool is in
C: or in Workbench's path:

    lha x "Work:Downloads/Game.lha"

    .lha .lzh   lha       .zip        unzip      .tar     tar
    .lzx        lzx       .tgz .tar.gz tar       .gz      gzip
    .7z         7z        .rar        unrar      .bz2 .xz bzip2, xz

Where it unpacks is "Unpack archives to" (@{"Shell drops and Keys" link advshell}):
the Shell's drawer, RAM:, or a drawer of your choice.

@{i}Works in the standard console and in others such as KingCON. OS 3.2's@{ui}
@{i}own Shell types paths itself, so there the dock leaves Shell windows@{ui}
@{i}alone. A console that types paths itself would show them twice: set@{ui}
@{i}"Icons into a Shell" to Off.@{ui}
@endnode

@node forcequit "Force Quit"
@toc main
@prev shelldrops
@next simple
@{b}@{u}Force Quit@{uu}@{ub}

A program that ignores Close keeps its long-press list; the second time
you open it, the list offers @{b}Force Quit...@{ub} It works in two steps:

  1. The @{b}break signals@{ub} (Ctrl+C, D, E, F) are sent. Shell programs and
     ports usually stop on those and clean up after themselves.
  2. Still there after two seconds, a requester asks. @{b}End it@{ub} removes
     the program's tasks (every copy, also one without a window) and
     closes its windows and its own screen.

Step 2 is the same as Scout's Remove, with the same risks: AmigaOS
tracks nothing a program holds, so its memory, files and locks stay in
use until a reboot, and a program ended inside a system call can take
the machine down. @{b}Save your work first.@{ub}

@{i}A Shell program ended this way no longer appears in Status.@{ui}
@endnode

@node simple "The simple side"
@toc main
@prev forcequit
@next finetune
@{b}@{u}The simple side@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}Dock@{ub} menu > @{b}Settings...@{ub} (on 3.5: Tools > SimpleDock Settings...).
Changes show on the dock at once and are saved when you close the
window. The dock stays shown while the window is open.

@{b}Place@{ub}
  Position         Top, Bottom, Left or Right
  Gap to edge      space between the dock and the screen edge
  Auto-hide        off, or how fast the dock hides
  In front         keep the dock in front of other windows
  Hotkey           the keys that show the dock: CTRL ALT SHIFT
                   (modifiers only: pressed together, let go), or a
                   combination with a key, such as CTRL ALT D

@{b}Icons@{ub}
  Icon size        own size, or shrunk to Small / Medium / Large
  Icon spacing     space between the icons
  Text             labels below the icons, on hover, off, or labels
                   only (no icons)

@{b}Look@{ub}
  Colour           six colours, or @{b}Custom...@{ub} for your own (a picker
                   with red, green and blue sliders)
  Transparent      Off: a solid dock. On: the screen shows through,
                   tinted with the colour
  Opacity          how dark the transparent dock is
  Corners          square to round

Click @{b}Advanced >>@{ub} for the rest, beside these (on a small screen, in
their place): @{"Fine-tune" link finetune}, @{"Programs" link programs},
@{"Shell drops and Keys" link advshell}, @{"System" link system}.

@{i}The keyword each setting has in the settings file is given in@{ui}
@{i}parentheses on those pages; you only need it for @{"Keywords" link keywords}.@{ui}
@endnode

@node finetune "Fine-tune"
@toc main
@prev simple
@next programs
@{b}@{u}Fine-tune@{uu}@{ub}

  Alignment        Centre, Start or End along the edge (POS)
  Offset           shift along the edge, pixels (OFFSET)
  Gap to edge      pixels (DISTANCE)
  Icon spacing     pixels (ICONDIST)
  Icon size        shrink icons bigger than this many pixels; 0 = own
                   size (SCALEICONW, SCALEICONH)
  Most icons       at most this many; 0 = as many as fit (LIMIT)
  Corner radius    pixels, 0-32 (RADIUS)
  Opacity          0-100 (OPACITY)
  Auto-hide speed  0 = off; a slide takes this x 70 ms (AUTOHIDE)
  Colour           #rrggbb (TINT)

@{i}The simple side and this one stay in step: Corners "Large" sets the@{ui}
@{i}radius to 18; a radius with no preset shows as "Other" there.@{ui}
@endnode

@node programs "Programs"
@toc main
@prev finetune
@next advshell
@{b}@{u}Programs@{uu}@{ub}

  Running programs Off, Dot, or Dot and number (RUNNING)
  Click when       Bring to front, or New copy - then Shift+click brings
  running          to the front instead (CLICKNEW)
  Start programs   SimpleDock (it then knows at once when a program
  with             ends), or Workbench, for a program that does not
                   like being started by the dock (WBSTART)
  Shell icons      Start at once, or Ask first: an "Execute a file"
                   requester with the command, as a double-click gives
                   (SHELLASK) - see @{"Shell icons and projects" link shellicons}
  Drop on a drawer Copy, or Like Workbench: moved when on the same disk
                   (DROPMOVE)
  Long press       Normal, Long or Longer: 0.3 / 0.4 / 0.5 s (HOLDTIME)
  Programs not on  Show or Hide the programs that are not on the dock
  the dock         (RUNHIDE)
  Workbench icon   Show or Hide the icon for the open drawer windows
                   (WBHIDE)
  Hidden programs  the programs you dragged off the dock, by name; pick
                   one and @{b}Remove@{ub} to see it again (IGNORE)

@{i}Programs get into Hidden programs by being dragged off the dock while@{ui}
@{i}they run (@{"Running programs" link running}); you never type a name there.@{ui}
@endnode

@node advshell "Shell drops and Keys"
@toc main
@prev programs
@next system
@{b}@{u}Shell drops and Keys@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}Shell drops@{ub} - see @{"Shell drops" link shelldrops}
  Icons into a     Type paths, or Off (SHELLDROP)
  Shell
  Unpack archives  the Shell's drawer, RAM:, or the Unpack drawer
  to               (UNPACKTO)
  Unpack drawer    that drawer; the @{b}...@{ub} button beside it opens a
                   drawer requester (UNPACKDIR)

@{b}Keys@{ub} - see @{"Keys" link keys}
  Minimise window  CTRL ALT M by default (MINKEY)
  Minimise all     CTRL ALT SHIFT M (MINALLKEY)
  Auto-hide on/off CTRL ALT H (AHKEY)

@{i}Type a key combination, or NONE to switch one off. Modifiers alone@{ui}
@{i}(CTRL ALT) are only allowed for the Hotkey.@{ui}
@endnode

@node system "System"
@toc main
@prev advshell
@next buttons
@{b}@{u}System@{uu}@{ub}

  Font             name/size for the labels; empty = the screen's (FONT)
  Screen           a public screen; empty = Workbench (PUBSCREEN)
  Start delay      wait this many 1/50 s before opening (DELAY)
  Messages         Normal; Quiet (no requesters at all); Verbose (also
                   what is normally silent) (QUIET, VERBOSE)
  Beep             On or Off: the beep on a refused action (NOBEEP)
  Crash reports    off by default; see @{"Crash reports" link crash} (CRASHLOG)
  No border        no thin border around the dock (NOBORDER)
@endnode

@node buttons "The buttons"
@toc main
@prev system
@next shellicons
@{b}@{u}The buttons@{uu}@{ub}

  @{b}Undo changes@{ub}   back to the settings the window opened with
  @{b}Load...@{ub}        another settings file's settings and icons
  @{b}Save as...@{ub}     a copy of yours to another file
  @{b}Advanced >>@{ub}    the rest of the settings; @{b}<< Simple@{ub} hides them

Numbers and text take effect on @{b}Return@{ub} or @{b}Tab@{ub}. A value that is not
valid, or one that would push icons off the dock, is refused with a
beep and the old value comes back.

@{i}SimpleDock always starts from its own file, ENVARC:SimpleDock.config,@{ui}
@{i}whatever you loaded or saved elsewhere.@{ui}
@endnode

@node shellicons "Shell icons and projects"
@toc main
@prev buttons
@next moredocks
@{b}@{u}Shell icons and projects@{uu}@{ub}

Icons set to @{b}start from a Shell@{ub} (the CLI tooltype), @{b}ARexx icons@{ub}
(REXX) and programs with @{b}no icon of their own@{ub} are handed to
Workbench, so they start as a double-click does.

With "Shell icons: Ask first" (@{"Programs" link programs}), the dock shows an
"Execute a file" requester first - not for icons with DONOTPROMPT.

A @{b}project's default tool@{ub} is found as Workbench finds it: in the
project's drawer, then in Workbench's path, then in C:.
@endnode

@node moredocks "More than one dock"
@toc main
@prev shellicons
@next configfile
@{b}@{u}More than one dock@{uu}@{ub}

You can run two docks at once, each with its own icons and settings -
say your programs along the bottom and your drawers along the right.

Make a second settings file (Save as... in the settings window, then
edit it), give it a project icon with SimpleDock as its default tool,
and double-click it: a second SimpleDock starts from that file and
opens its own dock. Its ARexx port is named after the file, so scripts
can address each dock on its own.

A dock can also be made from a @{b}drawer of icons@{ub} (CONFIG set to the
drawer): its settings live in its icon's tooltypes.
@endnode

@node configfile "The settings file"
@toc main
@prev moredocks
@next keywords
@{b}@{u}The settings file@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}ENVARC:SimpleDock.config@{ub} is plain text: settings lines, then one line
per icon. A keyword in parentheses is disabled.

    ; SimpleDock settings
    POS=B
    HOVERTEXT
    (AUTOHIDE=3)
    ; icons
    SYS:Utilities/MultiView
    LABEL Viewer
    SYS:System/Shell

@{b}LABEL@{ub} renames the icon before it; @{b}IMAGE@{ub} <path> borrows another
icon's picture.

The same keywords work as @{b}tooltypes@{ub} of the SimpleDock icon and on
the @{b}command line@{ub}: tooltypes, then the file, then the command line,
each overriding the one before. The keywords: @{"Keywords" link keywords}.
@endnode

@node keywords "Keywords"
@toc main
@prev configfile
@next arexx
@{b}@{u}Keywords@{uu}@{ub}

Settings file, tooltypes and command line alike.

  CONFIG=<file>       settings file (default ENVARC:SimpleDock.config)
  POS=<edge><align>   T, B, L or R, then C, S or E to align (POS=BS)
  DISTANCE=<n>        gap to the screen edge, pixels (default 4)
  OFFSET=<n>          shift along the edge, pixels
  ICONDIST=<n>        space between icons (default 2)
  SCALEICONW=<n>      shrink icons wider than this (0 = own size)
  SCALEICONH=<n>      shrink icons taller than this
  LIMIT=<n>           at most n icons (default: as many as fit)
  RADIUS=<n>          corner radius (default 12)
  NOTEXT              no labels
  HOVERTEXT           a label only under the pointer
  NOICON              labels only, no icons
  NOBORDER            no thin border
  FONT=<name/size>    label font
  TINT=#rrggbb        colour (default #383838)
  TRANSPARENT         tinted, see-through background
  OPACITY=<n>         its darkness, 0-100
  CUSTOMTINT=#rrggbb  the custom colour in the settings window's list
  AUTOHIDE=<n>        auto-hide; a slide takes n x 70 ms
  ONTOP               stay in front of other windows
  HOTKEY=<keys>       the hotkey (default ctrl alt shift)
  MINKEY=<keys>       minimise the active window (default ctrl alt m)
  MINALLKEY=<keys>    minimise all (default ctrl alt shift m)
  AHKEY=<keys>        auto-hide on/off (default ctrl alt h); none = off
  RUNNING=<n>         0 no dots, 1 dot, 2 dot and number (default 2)
  CLICKNEW            a click starts a new copy; Shift+click: to front
  WBSTART             Workbench starts the programs
  SHELLASK            Shell icons ask first
  DROPMOVE            drops onto a drawer move on the same disk
  HOLDTIME=<n>        long press, tenths of a second (default 3)
  RUNHIDE             programs not on the dock are not shown
  IGNORE=<names>      nor these ("Name,Name")
  WBHIDE              no Workbench icon
  SHELLDROP=<n>       1 icons dropped onto a Shell are typed (default),
                      0 off
  UNPACKTO=<n>        archives unpack: 0 the Shell's drawer, 1 RAM:,
                      2 UNPACKDIR
  UNPACKDIR=<drawer>  the drawer for UNPACKTO=2
  PUBSCREEN=<name>    the public screen to open on
  DELAY=<n>           wait n/50 s before opening
  QUIET / VERBOSE     fewer / more messages
  NOBEEP              no beep on a refused action
  CRASHLOG            crash reports (@{"Crash reports" link crash})
@endnode

@node arexx "ARexx"
@toc main
@prev keywords
@next limits
@{b}@{u}ARexx@{uu}@{ub}

The port is @{b}SIMPLEDOCK@{ub} (a second dock: its file's name). Every
command returns 0 = done, 5 = busy (an icon is being dragged: try
again), 10 = failed. -> marks a RESULT (OPTIONS RESULTS). An icon is
named by its label, file name or path, or #n for the nth.

@{b}The dock@{ub}
  SHOW / HIDE             open the dock again / close it (the dock
                          keeps running)
  REVEAL                  as the hotkey
  REFRESH                 reopen the dock
  GETPOS               -> "x y w h" of the dock, then of the icon
                          clicked last
  QUIT

@{b}Icons@{ub}
  LIST [NAMES]         -> the icons' paths (or labels), one per line
  ADD <path> [AT n | AFTER icon | BEFORE icon] [LABEL text]
  REMOVE <icon>
  MOVE <icon> AT n | AFTER icon | BEFORE icon

@{b}Settings@{ub}
  SET <keyword> ...       as the settings file; parentheses disable;
                          quote values with spaces
  GET [<keyword>]      -> its value (1 / 0 for a switch; empty: not
                          set); without a keyword: every setting
  SAVE [<file>]           settings and icons (to the dock's own file)
  RELOAD                  the dock's own file again
  LOAD <file>             another file's settings and icons
  SETTINGS [ADVANCED]     the settings window

@{b}For bug reports@{ub}
  DROPINFO / HOTKEYINFO -> what the dock sees of Shell windows / the
                          hotkey
  BENCH                -> drawing times on this machine

@{b}Examples@{ub}
    address SIMPLEDOCK 'ADD "RAM:" AFTER Shell LABEL "Ram Disk"'
    address SIMPLEDOCK 'SET AUTOHIDE=3 RADIUS=0'
    address SIMPLEDOCK 'SAVE'
    options results
    address SIMPLEDOCK 'GET AUTOHIDE'; say result
@endnode

@node limits "Known limitations"
@toc main
@prev arexx
@next crash
@{b}@{u}Known limitations@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}Running programs@{ub}
  AmigaOS keeps no list of running programs: the dock knows them by
  their windows and names. A program with no window, a renamed task or
  one started by a script is not seen; a click then starts a copy.
  A program the dock did not start is noticed when one of its windows
  becomes active, or within 2 seconds.
  Shell windows are found by asking each Shell's console; a console
  that does not answer gives a count but no window list.
  Minimise needs OS 3.2, or layers.library 45 (3.1.4, 3.9): the dock
  then hides the window's layers itself, and a program that brings its
  own window forward may show it again. Quitting the dock shows every
  minimised window.
  Close sends what the window's close gadget sends; a program may ask
  first, and a window without a close gadget cannot be closed (beep).
  Programs the dock started keep running when it quits; a few hundred
  bytes for each stay in memory until a reboot.
  Icons dropped straight into a KingCON window may not reach the dock
  (KingCON handles them itself); the dock's Shell icon does the job.

@{b}The dock@{ub}
  One row of icons. When it is full, the last place lists the rest.
  Windows cannot be round or see-through, so the corners and the
  transparent look use a picture of what is behind the dock. When that
  changes, the dock steps aside for a moment to take a new one.
  With auto-hide, icons cannot be dropped onto the hidden dock:
  Workbench locks the screen during a drag. Press the hotkey first.
  A hover label over a Shell window can leave a faint box behind.
  In front gives up after three tries in a row with nothing pressed in
  between (another window that keeps itself in front would fight it
  for ever) and rests while the settings window is open.
  A new screen mode needs every window closed: quit the dock first (or
  ARexx HIDE) and start it again after.

@{b}Older systems@{ub}
  Without ReAction's chooser.gadget (before 3.2) the settings lists
  are cycle gadgets: a click steps to the next choice.
  Marking a whole text field with a double-click needs 3.2.
  On 3.5 the Dock menu is in the Tools menu.

@{b}Settings@{ub}
  Version 1.0 does not know the settings new in 1.1 and shows them as
  icons marked "?". Remove those lines if you go back.
@endnode

@node crash "Crash reports"
@toc main
@prev limits
@next bugs
@{b}@{u}Crash reports@{uu}@{ub}

If SimpleDock crashes, switch @{b}Crash reports@{ub} on (@{"System" link system}) and
repeat what you did. The next crash writes
@{b}ENVARC:SimpleDock-crash.txt@{ub} - the error, where in the program, your
system and settings - shows a message and closes. Attach the file to
your report (@{"Bug reports" link bugs}).

@{i}Crash reports cost nothing while nothing goes wrong, and catch only@{ui}
@{i}errors in the dock's own task; if the whole system is unstable, reboot.@{ui}
@endnode

@node bugs "Bug reports"
@toc main
@prev crash
@next history
@{b}@{u}Bug reports@{uu}@{ub}

https://github.com/renewable-intel/SimpleDock/issues

Say what you did, step by step, and what happened; your CPU, graphics
card or AGA, and screen mode; and attach the crash report if there is
one (@{"Crash reports" link crash}).

For a Shell-drop or hotkey problem, the output of this helps:

    rx "options results; address SIMPLEDOCK 'DROPINFO'; say result"

(or HOTKEYINFO in place of DROPINFO).
@endnode

@node history "History"
@toc main
@prev bugs
@{b}@{u}History@{uu}@{ub}

@{b}1.1@{ub} (21.10.2026)
  Running programs: the dot and the count, click to front, Shift+click
  for a copy, Del to close; the long-press window list with Minimise,
  Show and Close; programs not on the dock shown behind a line; the
  Shell icon; the Workbench icon for open drawers; files dropped onto a
  drawer copied; the settings window in groups, with an Advanced side;
  Undo changes; the keys (minimise, minimise all, auto-hide); Shell
  drops and archive unpacking; Shell icons started as Workbench does,
  with an optional requester; Force Quit; Beep on/off; crash reports;
  the program a third smaller and lighter in memory; a code audit with
  18 fixes.

@{b}1.0@{ub} (15.10.2026)  First release.
@endnode

